<chapter xml:id="ch-data-management">
  <title>Data Management and Guidelines</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Handle student responses only through approved project systems.</li>
      <li>Protect identifiers without assuming that masked responses are anonymous.</li>
      <li>Preserve the provenance needed to reproduce every coding decision.</li>
      <li>Raise privacy, access, and analytic questions through the correct channel.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Student responses are both research evidence and potentially identifying records.
      Responsible work therefore has two inseparable aims. <term>Privacy stewardship</term>
      limits who can view, copy, transmit, or publish the data. <term>Provenance
      stewardship</term> preserves where each record came from, which analytic rules were
      used, and how an initial decision became a final one. A dataset that is secure but
      analytically untraceable is not research-ready. Neither is a perfectly documented
      dataset that has been handled through unapproved systems.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter gives the minimum practices supported by the current project record.
      It supplements the coding procedures in
      <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/> and the training sequence in
      <xref ref="ch-coding-methodology" text="title"/>. It does not replace the
      governing IRB protocol or institutional data-governance requirements.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-data-scope">
      <title>Local procedures must still be approved</title>
      <p>
        The source journal does not yet name one final shared storage location, complete
        access-control scheme, backup procedure, or retention schedule. Those choices
        remain research-team and institutional decisions. Until they are settled, do not
        improvise. Use only the accounts, folders, devices, and transfer methods explicitly
        approved for the current assignment.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-data-access">
    <title>Data Access and Privacy</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-approved-access">
      <title>Use Only Approved Systems</title>

      <p>
        Access to research data is granted for a specific project purpose. It does not
        authorize unrestricted copying or reuse. Student researchers must:
        <ul>
          <li>use only approved project accounts, folders, and devices;</li>
          <li>open only the files needed for the assigned work;</li>
          <li>avoid copying responses into personal documents or note-taking systems;</li>
          <li>avoid sending responses through personal email or messaging services;</li>
          <li>avoid placing responses in unapproved cloud storage; and</li>
          <li>follow any additional access restrictions attached to the dataset.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-identifiers">
      <title>Identifiers Reduce Exposure; They Do Not Guarantee Anonymity</title>

      <p>
        Use project identifiers rather than student names in coding sheets, notes, file
        names, tables, and discussion. Copy identifiers exactly. Do not replace them with
        memorable initials or informal nicknames, and do not attempt to infer the student
        behind an identifier.
      </p>

      <p>
        Masking a name is not the same as making a response anonymous. Free-text answers
        may contain names, personal experiences, or other self-identifying language.
        Course section, instructor, and timestamp fields may also make a record
        re-identifiable when combined. Treat the response text and its metadata as
        protected research data even when the visible student field is an opaque ID.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notes, presentations, screenshots, and demonstrations require the same care as
        the source file. Do not include identifying information in any of them. Use
        synthetic examples or explicitly approved excerpts when illustrating the method.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-external-services">
      <title>External Services Require Specific Approval</title>

      <p>
        Do not submit real student responses to a language model, chatbot, transcription
        service, translation service, or other third-party system unless you have approval. 
        Removing the student-name column is not by itself sufficient.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before any real response is routed to an external API, the research team must
        confirm the data-sharing basis with the IRB or institutional data-governance
        office and verify the provider conditions required by that approval. Until then,
        use hand-written synthetic responses for prompt development, demonstrations,
        debugging, and external-tool testing.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-data-files">
    <title>File and Identifier Practices</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-source-files">
      <title>Preserve the Source Record</title>

      <p>
        The raw export is the provenance anchor. Do not edit it to correct spelling,
        standardize IDs, remove rows, or make the file easier to read. Corrections and
        transformations belong in a documented derivative or in an approved upstream
        process. A later researcher must be able to determine exactly what the project
        received and how the working record was produced from it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The current project decision is that real short-answer exports remain local-only
        and untracked: they are never committed to the source repository because free-text
        responses may identify students.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-file-naming">
      <title>Name Files Without Adding Personal Information</title>

      <p>
        The journal does not yet define a universal file-name template. Until the team
        adopts one, use the naming convention supplied with the assignment and preserve
        canonical source names. New derivative names should describe the dataset or work
        stage without containing student names, initials, email addresses, or other direct
        identifiers. Avoid ambiguous names such as <c>final.csv</c>,
        <c>final2.csv</c>, or <c>newest.xlsx</c>; the associated record should make the
        dataset, stage, and version distinguishable.
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not rename a shared file merely to make it convenient for one local workflow.
        When a local alias or temporary copy is necessary, keep it in the approved working
        location and document which canonical file it represents.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-stable-identifiers">
      <title>Use Stable Identifiers Consistently</title>

      <p>
        Every coding record must retain the supplied <c>response_id</c> and
        <c>question_id</c>. The response identifier is the stable key used to connect the
        source response, episode rows, candidate codes, human reviews, and adjudicated
        result. Never substitute a row position from a sorted spreadsheet or a coder's own
        numbering system for the project identifier.
      </p>

      <p>
        Within a response, number episodes in the order required by the current coding
        protocol. Stable response and episode identifiers allow a disagreement note to
        point to the exact analytic unit without reproducing the student's text in an
        email or message.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-import-provenance">
      <title>Retain Import Provenance</title>

      <p>
        The ingest record carries context that supports later verification and duplicate
        detection. When available, preserve:
        <ul>
          <li>the original row number in the source export;</li>
          <li>the source file path or approved source label;</li>
          <li>the source-file hash recorded by the import process;</li>
          <li>the normalized submission timestamp;</li>
          <li>the course or section identifier; and</li>
          <li>the import identifier and import note.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        These fields are not invitations to expose more student information. They are
        provenance fields retained inside the approved project environment so that a
        questionable row can be traced to its source and a repeated import can be
        recognized rather than duplicated.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-data-reproducibility">
    <title>Reproducible Work</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-records-preserve">
      <title>Preserve the Full Decision Record</title>

      <p>
        A final code without its history cannot show whether agreement arose
        independently, through adjudication, or through silent revision. For every
        assigned batch, retain:
        <ul>
          <li>the response and question identifiers;</li>
          <li>the exact source response inside the approved data system;</li>
          <li>the handbook, protocol, codebook, and vocabulary versions;</li>
          <li>the coder's independent first-pass analysis;</li>
          <li>confidence and uncertainty notes;</li>
          <li>all disagreement classifications;</li>
          <li>the adjudicated result;</li>
          <li>the rationale for every override;</li>
          <li>proposed changes to coding materials; and</li>
          <li>the date and reviewer associated with each decision.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not replace an earlier interpretation by silently editing it. Preserve the
        proposal and the later decision. This makes coder drift, machine influence,
        adjudication, and framework revision available for analysis rather than erasing
        them from the record.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-version-discipline">
      <title>Record Versions Before Coding</title>

      <p>
        Confirm the governing versions before beginning a batch. A codebook or policy
        revision does not retroactively change the meaning of a record coded under an
        earlier version. When a rule changes, record the change, identify the affected
        artifacts, and decide explicitly whether earlier records require recoding.
      </p>

      <p>
        Formal validation requires a version freeze as described in
        <xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/>. Changing the screening rules,
        normative reference, codebook, vocabulary, nesting policy, or adjudication
        procedure during a held-out comparison can make measured disagreement reflect
        moving rules rather than differences among coders or systems.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-work-sessions">
      <title>Record Work Sessions and Coder Drift</title>

      <p>
        Coding conditions affect the data. Use focused sittings of approximately
        45-60 minutes rather than continuing through obvious fatigue. When coding time is
        part of the study, record the start and stop time in the required work log.
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not revisit earlier rows and silently change them after your interpretation of
        the rules shifts. For now, just record the suspected drift.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-repro-check">
      <title>Batch Completion Check</title>

      <p>
        Before submitting a batch, confirm that:
        <ol>
          <li>all required records use stable project identifiers;</li>
          <li>the governing versions are present;</li>
          <li>low-confidence decisions include a specific note;</li>
          <li>alternate interpretations and no-match cases remain visible;</li>
          <li>earlier proposals have not been overwritten;</li>
          <li>the batch contains no student names or copied identifiers in notes; and</li>
          <li>the files remain inside the approved project location.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-data-communication">
    <title>Questions, Notes, and Escalation</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-independent-questions">
      <title>Protect Independent Coding</title>
      
      <p>
        When a worksheet requires independent work, follow its communication restrictions.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-useful-question">
      <title>Write a Question That Can Be Resolved</title>

      <p>
        Analytic questions and disagreement notes should use the evidence-based 
        format in <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-notes" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-data-escalation-types">
      <title>Match the Escalation to the Problem</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-data-escalation-types">
        <title>Common escalation routes.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="25%"/>
          <col width="42%"/>
          <col width="33%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Problem</cell>
            <cell>Immediate action</cell>
            <cell>Record to preserve</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Possible privacy or access incident</cell>
            <cell>Stop the affected work.</cell>
            <cell>What occurred, when, which system or file was involved, and what actions were already taken.</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Unapproved storage or external-service request</cell>
            <cell>Do not upload or transfer the data. Request a project-level decision.</cell>
            <cell>The proposed system, intended use, data involved, and approval question.</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      Good stewardship makes the research trustworthy in two directions. It protects
      students by limiting access, disclosure, and unapproved transmission. It protects
      the analysis by preserving source provenance, governing versions, independent
      judgments, disagreements, and revisions. Every research assistant contributes to
      both responsibilities whenever they open a response, enter a code, save a file, or
      ask for help.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>